  "recommendations": [
    "Maintenance is 51.1% of all work orders (4,199 of 8,224) and Common Areas another 32.6% (2,680). Together they are 84% of volume — a preventive maintenance program targeting the most repeated items is the single highest-leverage change available.",
    "HVAC Routine has an average completion time of 70.2 days and 31 of the 90 currently open work orders (34%) — the worst ratio of any category. Routine HVAC is being opened and then parked. Batch it into scheduled seasonal service runs with a hard close-out date.",
    "\"To do\" averages 102.6 days to close across 25 work orders. This category is functioning as a holding bin rather than a queue. Either retire it or apply the same SLA as Maintenance.",
    "14 open work orders are over 90 days old and 9 more are 60-90 days. The oldest, #37307 \"Door jamb\" at 824-826 Chicopee St, has been open 987 days and is flagged High priority with no status set. Age out or close the pre-2025 backlog before it grows.",
    "Yellow Brick Property carries 3,872 work orders (47% of portfolio volume) and 38 of 90 open items. Its 17.5-day average completion is portfolio-typical, so this is a scale issue, not a performance issue — staffing should be allocated proportionally.",
    "Flying Monkey has the slowest average completion at 23.6 days on only 116 work orders, and Courageous Lion is second at 19.0 days on 1,858. Both sit well above the 17.0-day portfolio average and are worth a routing review.",
    "Allyn St closed all 492 work orders with zero open and a 10.6-day average — the best performance in the portfolio. Whatever the dispatch practice is there, it is the internal benchmark to copy.",
    "Only 9.6% of work orders close within 24 hours and 23.0% within 72 hours, but 50.6% close within a week. Response is clustering at the 4-7 day mark; moving even a third of that into 72 hours would materially change tenant experience.",
    "6,590 work orders (80%) are repeats — the same property and category recurring inside 90 days. This is the strongest argument in the dataset for root-cause repair over patch-and-return.",
    "Volume peaks in January (avg 151) and October (avg 143) and bottoms in May (avg 121) and November (avg 121). Schedule preventive and turnover work into the May and November troughs to flatten the January spike."
  ],
